#{[17592186510470 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510247 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510470 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118917" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510470 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510470 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/60/609b6e03ee437023674cffc3edd76d6f6d3bc8e10787e1aa20b360f70df3ec48.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius\n$\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}}{2},$ as shown above. What is the greatest possible perimeter of the triangle?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510470 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ecb03265-da68-58e1-a086-fdf2d2ccd57f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510470 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(l) & =5\\sqrt{2}+l+\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $5\\sqrt{2},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<5\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{-2l}{\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{50-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =50-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =50 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =25 \\\\ l & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=5$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,5)$ | $5$ | $(5,5\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $6$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=5$ does indeed give a maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5) & =5\\sqrt{2}+5+\\sqrt{50-{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}+5+\\sqrt{50-25} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}+5+\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}+5+5 \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{2}+10 \\\\  & =5(2+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510475 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186510343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510475 #edb/ref 1069 "q-18643" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510475 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510475 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/ea/ea0b899d48da746716c143eaa22e497250043a43ccc79b6662067f4046510613.png)\n\nConsider a right triangle inscribed in a semicircle of radius $3,$ as shown above. Find the maximum possible area of the triangle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510475 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3df6436-2be0-5dd6-ab61-56461a3aff28" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186510475 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's call $l$ the length of one leg. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{36-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the area of the triangle is given by\n\n$$\nA(l)=\\frac{1}{2}l\\sqrt{36-{l}^{2}}\\,.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $6$, the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<6.$\n\nWe want to maximize $A(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{36-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{36-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving $A(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{36-{l}^{2}}-\\frac{{l}^{2}}{2\\sqrt{36-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ 36-{l}^{2}-{l}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =\\frac{36}{2} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =18 \\\\ l & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=3\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,3\\sqrt{2})$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $(3\\sqrt{2},6)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $5$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=3\\sqrt{2}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nFinally, the maximum possible area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(3\\sqrt{2}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{36-{(3\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
